## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:
Nanobiocatalysis  Magnetic materials  MOFs  Enzyme immobilization  Biotransformation
Nanobiocatalysts, in which enzyme molecules are integrated into/onto multifunctional materials, such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), have been fascinating and appeared as a new interface of nanobiocatalysis with multi-oriented applications. Among various nano-support matrices, functionalized MOFs with magnetic attributes have gained supreme interest as versatile nano-biocatalytic systems for organic bio-transformations. From the design (fabrication) to deployment (application), magnetic MOFs have manifested notable efficacy in manipulating the enzyme microenvironment for robust biocatalysis and thus assure requisite applications in several areas of enzyme engineering at large and nano-biocatalytic transformations, in particular. Magnetic MOFs-linked enzyme-based nano-biocatalytic systems offer chemo-regio- and stereo-selectivities, specificities, and resistivities under fine-tuned enzyme microenvironments. Considering the current sustainable bioprocesses demands and green chemistry needs, we reviewed synthesis chemistry and application prospects of magnetic MOFs-immobilized enzyme-based nano-biocatalytic systems for exploitability in different industrial and biotechnological sectors. More specifically, following a thorough introductory background, the first half of the review discusses various approaches to effectively developed magnetic MOFs. The second half mainly focuses on MOFs-assisted biocatalytic transformation applications, including biodegradation of phenolic compounds, removal of endocrine disrupting compounds, dye decolorization, green biosynthesis of sweeteners, biodiesel production, detection of herbicides and screening of ligands and inhibitors.
## 1. Introduction
Enzymes are an integral part of biological transformations in diverse fields, i.e., chemical, pharmaceutical, as well as food productions, because of their high efficiency and selectivity [1,2]. Despite all these benefits, enzymes are frequently hampered by a lack of chemical resistance, recovery, and recyclability. It has been shown that immobilizing the enzymes onto solid or insoluble supports may solve a fraction of these problems since it makes the enzymes more accessible in terms of
separation, storage, recycling, and stability at extreme temperatures and pH [3,4]. Immobilization allows the solid form of the enzyme to be sustained, which improves handling; otherwise, it will create problems in liquid enzyme formulations [5,6]. Immobilized enzymes are more stable and can withstand adverse storage and use conditions, including denaturation by heat, organic solvents, or autolysis. Immobilization makes it easier for enzymes to be separated and used again, which decreases or eliminates protein contamination in the reaction medium. This greatly lowers costs, enabling industrial application in continuous
operation [7]. Different ways have been introduced for immobilizing enzymes on various supports [8], including covalent binding [9], entrapment [10], physical adsorption [11], and the De novo encapsulation method [12]. Zdarta et al. and Chao et al. reviewed enzyme immobilization on solid supports [13,14]. It has been documented in previous research that porous materials are excellent solid support systems for enzyme immobilization. Because porous materials can be encapsulated, separated, and recovered, they are ideal for enzyme immobilization. Different solid supports are bulk materials [15], particles [16], hydrogels [17], carbon nanotubes [18], graphene oxide [19], etc. Since the physical and chemical characteristics of enzymes may change during the immobilization process, it is crucial to understand the matrix and techniques in order to maximize the activity and stability of the immobilized enzyme [20]. Therefore, MOFs are unique porous materials in several ways, i.e., greater surface areas, crystalline structure, huge pore volumes, pore diameters that can be altered through well-defined active sites, and the ability to hold organic ligands [21,22]. The topology and characteristics of MOFs can be altered to accommodate a wide variety of metal nodes and ligands, which makes it possible for MOF supports and enzymes to form bonds of many types, including van der Waals forces, hydrogen bonding, covalent bonding, and coordinative [23]. With large pore volume and amenability to modification, MOFs have recently emerged as a prodigious matrix for the immobilization of enzymes, as they have a lot of exposed surfaces [8,24–26]. MOFs have been created for use as enzyme carriers; examples include HKUST-1, UiO-66, MIL-100Fe, PCN-888, and ZIF-8. These characteristics of the MOF biocomposites produced by this technique include the capability of withstanding high temperatures and a wide range of organic solvents without degradation (Fig. 1) [8,27,28]. In addition, the investigated potential of MOFs as supports for immobilization of the enzyme can be evaluated. [29] synthesized hierarchical ZIF-8 to immobilize cytochrome \(c\) (Cyt-c) and showed that its apparent support probability and enzymatic action were higher than free Cyt c. Liu et al. [30] and Wu, Yang et al. [31] utilized the amino acid histidine (His) and cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) as templates to construct hierarchically porous MOF. Moreover, the immobilized lysozyme protein shows remarkable resistance to temperature changes. However, recycling composites is extremely difficult because the nanoscale extent of enzyme-MOF bio-composites have a high degree of solvent solubility [31,32]. Therefore, incorporating magnetic microspheres (for example, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)) into the MOFs carriers is a potential solution to this issue [32,33]. Magnetic materials have attracted the attention of many researchers because of their unique properties, i.e., excellent adsorption capabilities, extraordinary functioning, finally, straightforward recycling through any external magnetic field. Although, the amalgamation of the MOFs with magnetic nanoparticles (NPs) been the main focus of our finding. Magnetic-MOFs have gained incredible interest in the new era because it
offers fascinating properties of porosity and magnetism to constitute new materials with substantial surface area and easier magnetic-assisted recovery from the complex mixture. Therefore, these properties of MMOFs made them substantially different from other materials. The magnetic-supported MOF has the most evident potential benefit in separating and managing MOFs embedded with enzymes [34]. Additionally, they offer several distinctive qualities, such as a large surface area (in comparison to bare magnetic nanoparticles), simple enzyme loading, quick collection from reaction mixtures, and compositional flexibility. These complimentary properties of magnetic MOFs coupled with enzymes enhanced the overall activity in the reaction mixture [35,36]. Because of the complementary effects of MOFs, combining magnetic nanoparticles (MNPs) with MOFs has garnered a substantial amount of focus to enhance the performance of MOFs [37]. Magnetism can be used to alleviate the limits of material recycling. A superparamagnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticle (NPs) is an attractive option because of its high saturation magnetic moment. Since the discovery of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs as peroxidase mimics studied in 2007 [38,39], there has been a rise in the number of articles written on nanomaterial-based artificial enzymes (nanozymes). Some of the examples are iron-doped \(\mathrm{CuSn(OH)}_{6}\) (microspheres) [40], Gox &HRP/ZIF-8 nano-composite [41], Ce-UiO-66 MOF [42], \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53(Fe) [43,44], Ce/Fe@ NPC-GF [44], gold NPs [45,46], CaP@Fe-MOFs [46].
This review explores the scientific progress and development of magnetic MOFs as enzymatic immobilization support. A particular focus has been given to MOFs-assisted biocatalytic transformation applications, including biodegradation of phenolic compounds, removal of endocrine disrupting compounds, dye decolorization, green biosynthesis of sweeteners, biodiesel production, detection of herbicides and screening of ligands and inhibitors.
## 2. Metal-organic framework
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have become a promising hybrid organic/inorganic crystalline material useful in a variety of contexts, such as catalysis, energy conversion/storage drug/enzyme carriers, biomedical sciences, and separation [47–54]. Magnetic metal-organic framework nanocomposites are innovative, effective materials that have evolved recently [55]. Magnetic NPs coupled with porous MOFs make up the composition of magnetic MOFs (MMOFs). It also maintains the structure and functionality of the MOF material and enhances the magnetic properties of the granular material. With the advantages of great selectivity, excellent dispersion, and several reuses, it may be recovered and separated using a magnet from a mixture of soil or aqueous solution. Using an external magnetic field, magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) may be easily isolated from reaction liquids. Controlling the formation of MOF crystals on magnetic nanoparticles is very difficult. Few Magnetic
framework composite syntheses have been documented in the literature. According to Li et al., MOF-5@SiO2@Fe3O4 core-shell magnetic catalysts were synthesized by covering SiO2@Fe3O4 NPs with regular MOF5. The results show that the magnetic separation of the magnetic core-shell nanocomposites in their as-prepared state from the mixed reaction system is straightforward [56]. The creation of magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}\) was also documented by Zhao et al., who also verified its usage as an enzyme immobilization vector. The polydopamine (PDA) layer functionalized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs, and then \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\) was synthesized on the surface of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}\) by immersing \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}\) in an ethanol solution containing copper acetate and 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylic acid. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]\)-enzyme composites achieved excellent digestion efficiency, reusability, durability, and reproducibility due to their high affinity for the enzyme [57].
Magnetic-MOF exhibits enzyme-like properties that mimic the structures and functions of natural enzymes. Nanozymes are the common name for these structures. However, employing nanozymes has a few downsides, such as it is difficult to recover them after their utilization and the limited number of times they may be used. To overcome these challenges, magnetic NP-supported nanozymes have gained recognition because of their numerous desirable features, including high stability, low cost, ecological, simple recovery through an external magnet, and recyclability. Magnetic MOFs Enzyme is a type of effective biocatalyst that originated naturally. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1\)-Trypsin nanocomposites were developed by [57] using a technique for synthesizing complex molecules in several steps. In this method, the electrostatic contact was used to immobilize trypsin on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1\) NPs after their synthesis. In a previous study, Hou et al. developed a calorimetric biosensor called mIF-8@GOx by combining mimetic peroxidase of magnetic zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (mZIF-8) with embedded GOx to construct a mimic multi-enzyme system for the detection of glucose [58].
## 3. Synthetic approaches to magnetic MOFs
Physical or chemical processes mix MOFs with MNPs (such as ferrites, alloys, metal oxides, etc.) to create magnetic MOFs. It not only maintains the characteristics of MOFs, such as their porousness, adaptability, and high specific surface area, but also endows them with the capacity for magnetic separation. MOFs are aided by bulk transfer, diffusion, and confinement because of their consistent pore design, which stops analytes from aggregating into larger clusters. Fig. 2 represents the synthetic approaches of magnetic-MOFs nanostructures.
## 3.1. Self-template approach
The composites of magnetic metals or metal oxides will be responsible for donating the metal ions. After completing the method, these metal ions will coordinate with the organic ligand. Using the self-template technique, it is possible to synthesize \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1\) magnetic core-shell composite by first coating magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) with a \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) shell as a sacrificial template and then allowing HKUST-1 to grow around the core-shell. This would be done using the self-template technique [59,60]. Huang et al. used \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) as a self-template to coat \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and then used ethanol and water as solvents to transform \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) into HKUST-1 at ambient temperature. Following its successful application as an MSPE adsorbent, the MMOFs composite \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1\) developed a highly attuned, precise and user-friendly MSPE technique for the detection of \(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}\) in water [59]. This method has a substantial cost advantage over alternative template systems and does not require extra surface change. Cai and colleagues also used this technique to report a unique magnetic Prussian blue (PB) composite [61]. To create the \(\mathrm{PB}-\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) combination, a PB cube was sacrificed and employed as a source of iron. Using self-sacrificing magnetic Rh-Ni-alloyed nanoflowers (NFs) as a precursor, Chen and colleagues reported a MOF-74(Ni)-encapsulated Rh-Ni hierarchical hetero-structure (Rh-Ni@MOF-74(Ni)) [62]. The encapsulated state of the generated MOFs could be finely controlled, and this was accomplished by altering the amount of Ni present in the Rh-Ni NPs template. Remarkably, the selective hydrogenation of alkynes to cis products showed increased catalytic activity when Rh-Ni@MOF-74 (Ni) was used as the catalyst.
## 3.2. Layer-by-layer self-assembly method
Layer-by-layer (LbL) self-assembly has gained popularity among researchers because of its facile and effective surface modification process. This has contributed to the growth of material preparation in previous research [63,64]. Before combining with the ligand solution of MOFs, MMOFs preparation modifies functional groups on magnetic nanomaterials. This permit MOFs ligands to expand on magnetic nanomaterials and spontaneously form of MMOFs [65,66]. In general, the functional groups modified on the surface of magnetic nanomaterials can encourage the growth of MOFs crystal nuclei. This improves MMOFs core-shell structure and stability [67,68]. LbL self-assembly approach makes \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\mathrm{BA}\)-functional MOFs for extremely specific flavone detention and isolation from complex extraction materials [69]. Liu et al. used a MOF-\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1/\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})\) magnetic composite to detect methylene blue in food [70]. Miao and coworkers developed the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{P4VP}\) core as well as using LbL self-assembly to develop the MIL-100(Fe) shell [71]. This magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{P4VP}@\mathrm{MIL}-100(\mathrm{Fe})\) core–shell composite was used to selectively oxidize alcohols (maintained after ten reuses).
By varying the number of self-assembly cycles, the LbL self-assembly approach can also precisely control the thickness or other characteristics of MMOFs while maintaining relatively benign reaction conditions. However, this method has a disadvantage, it frequently takes a long time to build MMOFs to the appropriate thickness, and there are only a few different kinds of self-assembled MOF ligands, which restricts the creation of new MOFs with the crystal structure.
## 3.3. Hybrid preparation method
In ultrasonic or high-temperature polymerization, the pre-prepared MNPs are incorporated with the MOFs, producing MMOF composites [72,73]. This method is suitable for any kind of MMOFs preparation with strong applicability, and their electrostatic or chemical bonding can modify MNPs on MOFs. Adjusting MNP-MOF interactions can also affect MMOF structure and performance. To develop an electrostatic composite, Jiang et al. ultrasonically blended cysteine functionalized
\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs with MIL-101(Fe) [74]. The magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{MIL}-101(\mathrm{Fe})\) is simpler to isolate from water. Since various active centres work together in a synergistic peroxidase-like manner, compared to the isolated substance, it catalyzes more effectively \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs and MIL-101). To increase the hybrid microcrystal stability and homogeneity, aminofunctionalized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs were chemically bonded to MOF-5. PeaMéndez et al. used a hybrid approach to manufacture \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Fe}\) - (benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid)) [75]. However, this approach has several limitations, similar to the weak MNP and MOF combination that quickly separates from one another. Additionally, producing enormous volumes of MOFs in advance frequently necessitates greater production costs [76].
## 3.4. In situ growth
This method allows MOFs already synthesized to be dissolved in the reagents used to develop MNPs. Therefore, MNPs will be prepared on the surface of the MOFs. This method has many advantages, such as MOF growth on MNPs can be accomplished in one step and is rather straightforward. It is ideal for industrial production since this preparation procedure may be carried out at room temperature, negating the requirement for labor-intensive carbonization procedures at high temperatures and considerably decreasing preparation difficulty and time [77]. sing centrifugation, unneeded MNPs are separated; a magnetic field can do the same for MMOFs and basic MOFs. The manufacturing of a magnetic hybrid \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{MIL}-101\) composite using an in-situ MNP growth method was investigated in recent research [78]. MIL-101 and \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\left(2/1\right)\) are combined in n-octane, and after that, an ammonia solution was used to precipitate \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs on the crystal surface of MIL101. Different characterization techniques of produced \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{MIL}-101\) composite revealed that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs are evenly deposited on MIL-101's exterior surface. This composite outperforms MIL-101 in terms of surface area, high porosity, and positive charge, making it an excellent material for adsorbing and removing anionic dye pollutants from aqueous media. The MMOFs produced by the in-situ growth of MNPs approach feature an MMOF structure that is significantly more complete, as well as superior adsorption, and has magnetic properties [79,80].
However, this method also has some drawbacks; it is difficult to avoid the direct nucleation and growth of MOFs in solution when MOFs are grown in situ on MNPs because a portion of the MNPs is lodged in the pores of the MOFs, which reduces the specific surface area [81,82]. In addition, the presence of a significant amount of unreacted magnetic nanoparticles in the reaction solution has complicated the subsequent separation stages and increased the need for organic solvents [83].
## 3.5. Dry gel conversion method
First, the reactants were removed from the solvent and steamed to develop MOFs around MNPs [84,85]. [86] proposed preparing MMOFs by dry gel conversion. At a high temperature, the solid was strewed with \(N,N\)-dimethylformamide to induce MOF development around MNPs, resulting in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{HKUST}-1\). Growing around MNPs eliminates material pores blocked by unreacted MNPs in created MOF material channels, decreases organic solvent loss, and reduces preparation time. It is important to note that the MMOFs created using this method need greater consistency. Due to its straightforward synthesis procedure and low cost, this mechanical grinding method also shows significant potential in upcoming investigations on the manufacture of MMOFs, despite the limited variety of MOF ligands accessible and the uneven characteristics of the generated MMOFs.
## 3.6. Pre-synthesized method
In order to develop MOFs, previously synthesized magnetic NPs are used. The magnetic NPs that have already been manufactured need to be
functionalized first. Li et al. have described MOF-5@SiO\(_2\)\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) core-shell magnetic catalysts [56]. A magnet may quickly and easily separate magnetic core-shell nanocomposites that have already been manufactured. Zhao et al. synthesized magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}\) to immobilize enzymes [57]. The polydopamine (PDA) layer was employed to functionalize \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs, and \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\) was prepared on the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}\) surface by adding it to a solution of 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylic acid and copper acetate in ethanol. MOF shells can withstand a lot of weight due to their large surface area. Strong affinities for the enzyme \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PDA}@\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]\) enzyme components have a high degree of reproducibility, robustness, and efficacy in digestion.
## 4. Magnetic–MOFs for enzyme immobilization
Immobilization of enzymes onto magnetic–MOF can generally be accomplished by four strategies (i) physical adsorption onto magneticMOF, (ii) covalent attachment to magnetic–MOF, (iii) coordination coupling, and (iv) de novo encapsulation. During enzyme immobilization, various metal ions are utilized as an inorganic component, whereas \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\), and 2-methylimidazole are widely organic linking agents to form MOF around the MNPs (generally \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)).
## 4.1. Physical adsorption
Physical adsorption is a cost-effective, simpler, and faster route widely applied to immobilize biological macromolecules and enzymes. Among various porous nonarchitectures, MOFs have appeared as a prospective carrier for enzyme immobilization given their substantial surface area, pore diameters, and uniform pore size. MOFs are likely to create robust hydrophobic, hydrophilic, and electrostatic interactions with enzyme molecules that help prevent their leakage during the operational process [87]. A new magnetic Ni-based MOF was constructed by a facile one-pot hydrothermal process for efficient separation and immobilization of thermostable S-adenosylmethionine synthetase (SAMS) with superior biocatalytic efficiency. Though \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{BTC}\) presented a preference for a histidine-tagged enhanced green fluorescent protein, a number of other forces were also found to play a critical role in enzyme immobilization, such as electrostatic attraction, hydrogen bonding, the affinity between histidine tags and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\), and hydrophobic forces. Under an elevated temperature (70 °C), the immobilized SAMS showed 95% activity recovery in biosynthesizing S-adenosylmethionine. It was 1.3- and 8-fold more stable at a highly acidic pH of 3.0 and 80 °C for 2 h and demonstrated appreciable reuse capability [88]. Laccase enzyme immobilized on amino-functionalized magnetic MOF, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL}-101(\mathrm{Cr})\) by simple adsorption showed extensive immobilization capacity, high recovered activity, and excellent resistance to high temperature and low pH environments. After 28 days of storage, it retained over 85% of its original activity and demonstrated 49.1% remaining activity after 6 h of preservation at 85 °C. The steadiness of adsorbed enzymes toward organic solvents, including methanol, was also notably enhanced [89].
## 4.2. Covalent bonding
In the adsorption process, there is a possibility of the enzyme escaping from the supporting material resulting in lower functional stability of the enzyme. Covalent immobilization by multi-point stabilization can lead to enhanced catalytic performance and operational stability [90]. It also imparts outstanding recyclability owing to chemical binding with the support material [91]. In a typical post-synthetic modification, a large number of free and reactive functional groups (e.g., amino, carboxyl, hydroxyl groups) are incorporated onto MOF surfaces, which can be triggered to facilitate coupling with functional groups on the enzyme surface [92].
## 4.3. Metal–ion affinity coordination bonding
Metal-ion affinity-based enzyme immobilization portrays a relatively interesting technique for selective enzyme adsorption on the surface-exposed metal ion clusters of magnetic MOFs [93]. The attachment of an enzyme relies on the interactions between electron-donating groups on the surface of protein/enzyme and metal ion via one or more coordination binding [94].
## 4.4. De novo encapsulation method
Covalent bonding, physical adsorption, and metal-ion affinity coordination bonding methods implicate enzyme immobilization after forming magnetic MOFs. In these post-synthetic immobilization approaches, enzymes are directly incorporated into MOF pores using coordination binding or physical adsorption that restricts the immobilization because of the large enzyme size than the tiny aperture and pores size of MOFs. Hou et al. developed an alternative strategy based on biomineralization, in which biomolecules were encapsulated within the MOF by an easy de novo method without substantially impacting the intrinsic porosity of MOFs. Mixing the enzyme, MOF precursors (ligands, metal ions, and additives), and citrate-coated MNPs in a solution result in crystallized magnetically responsive MOF derivatives with embedded enzymes. The de novo approach can address the major limitations associated with other methodologies because the magnetic MOFs are prepared at mild conditions to preserve the structure and catalytic efficiency of the enzyme [95]. This is one of the main motives for increasing interest in zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), which can be readily prepared under mild biocompatible settings [96]. [97] reported that magnetic MOF-embedded Bacillus protease KHB3 presented the maximum activity at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) along with a \(20~\%\) activity enhancement at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) compared to the free form of the enzyme. Compared to the control, it displayed 1.54 and 1.55-fold greater activity for fibrin and gelatin, respectively. It also exhibited \(38\,\%\) clot lysis, whereas the free state of the enzyme caused \(26\,\%\) under the same conditions. Results revealed the robust use of immobilized protease in the clot lysis and production of value-added compounds from the hydrolyzed protein of shrimp waste. A self-assembly strategy effectively encapsulated cobalt-type AT-DAEase into the cobalt-based magnetic MOF ZIF- \(67@{\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) . With reference to free AT-DAEase, the AT-DAEase@ZIF- \(67@{\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) NPs displayed superior catalytic activity and bioconversion efficiency \((38.1\ \ \%)\) . The developed nanobiocatalytic system displayed significantly enhanced thermal resistance and outstanding recycling efficiency retaining \(80~\%\) of catalytic activity at \(45{-}70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and over \(45\;\%\) of its original activity after 8 successive reuse cycles [98].
## 5. Enzyme-immobilized magnetic MOFs and their advantages
Gao et al. used solvothermal synthesis to grow Fe-MOF around \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs, producing a core-shell \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Fe-MOF}\) with a scaled-up pore diameter structure. The magnetic properties of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Fe-MOF}\) are integrated with the hierarchical porous structure that is necessary for supporting either chloroperoxidase (CPO) or horseradish peroxidase (HRP). The immobilized CPO/HRP showed dramatically improved resistance to temperature stress and made it easier to reuse. For instance, HRP- \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Fe}\) -MOF can keep \(94.1\ \%\) of its activity after being reused ten times. Furthermore, CPO/HRP- \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Fe}\) -MOFs were particularly effective in treating organic pollutants, such as isoproturon or 2,4- dichlorophenol in simulated wastewater within \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) [99].
Xu et al. synthesized core-shell-structured \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-COOH@UiO-66-}\) \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) for PPL immobilization. The advantages of a high specific surface area, an extensive active-sites concentration, and a superparamagnetic characteristic were all present in the synthesized composite. When conditions were ideal, the PPL load quantity was \(247.8~\mathrm{mg/g}\) , and the probable activity robustness was \(101.5\ \%\) . There was an increase in
immobilized enzyme stability. In particular, a practical technique was established based on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-COOH@UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) @PPL and UPLCQTOF-MS/MS for identifying bioactive chemical natural substances. Using the aforementioned strategy, eight inhibitors were tested and isolated from the S. baicalensis used in traditional Chinese medicine [100].
In order to magnetize a Ce-BDC MOF, Zhang et al. used an in situ generated \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) -based nano-magnetic fluid (BDC 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid). They did so by magnetizing the Ce-BDC metal-organic framework (MOF). The mixed-valence MOFs that were created using 3,3',5,5 Ce-BDC @Fe3O4 can convert tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) into oxTMB without using \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) . The catalytic activity of Ce-BDC MOF alone is 0.25 times lower than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nano-magnetic fluid when the same conditions apply. This demonstrates how synergy caused a significant improvement in catalytic efficiency [101]. By putting the Aspergillus flavus urate oxidase (Uox) enzyme on the surface of a magnetic MOF (Nim-MOF) nanomaterial, Motamedi et al. improved the enzyme's activity. The immobilized Uox@NimMOF enzyme maintained around \(60\;\%\) of its initial function and even had some activity at \(70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{:}\) while the unbound Uox enzyme nearly dropped its entire activities at \(40{-}45\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . After stabilization, the enzyme's activation energy (Ea) was determined to be \(58.81\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) . This is around half of the value for the naked Uox enzyme [102].
Wei et al. designed reusable and magnetic MOFs to specifically immobilize His-tagged enzymes from cell lysates without the need for purification. Compared to its native form, immobilized \(\upbeta\) -glucuronidase was more pH and temperature-stable. After eight cycles, immobilized \(\upbeta\) -glucuronidase still had \(80\ \%\) of its initial relative activity. The immobilization capability of regenerated MOFs increased to greater than \(90\,\%\) following only three repetitions of the basic treatment. Using the targeted magnetic MOFs for the one-step immobilization and purification of His-tagged enzymes has shown to be an effective and sustainable system with significant promise for use in industrial nanotechnology and biocatalysis [103]. In a research study, Sargazi et al. synthesized Ta-MOF @Fe3O4 core/shell nanostructures in the best possible conditions by employing a unique method that is rapid and effective and uses ultrasonic to assist in the reverse micelle reaction. On the Ta-MOF@ \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) substrate, \(\mathrm{Km}12\) lipase from Bacillus licheniformis was immobilized. This novel nanostructure provides a great solid substrate for lipase immobilization, because it possesses a large surface area, free carboxylate groups, and unsaturated metal centers. In contrast to the unbound enzyme, the immobilized form of lipase exhibited considerably better enzyme stability and continued activity even when exposed to high substrate concentrations. Finally, this Ta-MOF@Fe3O4- lipase has the potential to be used in biotechnology for applications such as biotransformation, the manufacture of biodiesel, and other similar endeavors [104].
Recently, Ji et al. researchers efficaciously encapsulated lipase (CRL) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs inside ZIF-8 using a mild and speedy one-pot approach, yielding CRL/MNP@ZIF-8 with an immobilization yield of \(88.4\ \%\) . MNPs and lipase have been encapsulated in ZIF-8 for the first time via a co-precipitation method. The resultant magnetic MOF composite showed extraordinary abilities for protecting against extreme circumstances and has the potential to simplify difficult downstream separation and reclamation aspects. Furthermore, CRL/MNP@ZIF-8 had \(82\ \%\) remaining activity after being used to hydrolyze \(p\) -nitrophenyl acetate for five consecutive cycles, which has the potential to significantly lower processing costs and may one day be used in industries [105].
Li and colleagues, encapsulated large-substrate enzymes in CaBDCMOF layers coated on MNPs using an enzyme-friendly, aqueous-phase one-pot synthesis in this study. \(\mathrm{{Ca^{2+}}}\) and terephthalic acid were used to achieve this (BDC). The composite size can be modified by utilizing MNPs as crystallization nuclei. When \(\mathrm{Ca^{2+}/B D C}\) was present in low concentrations, nanoscale composites were created, whereas microscale composites were formed when \(\mathrm{Ca^{2+}/B D C}\) was present in high concentrations. The nanoscale composites outperformed a stiff, crystalline-like
big substrate, starch, in terms of catalytic efficiency. This was most likely owing to the nanoscale composites' enhanced diffusivity. In contrast, the microscale composites demonstrated dramatically improved reusability when compared to a big unstructured substrate. We are aware of no other work that discusses the production of size-adjustable enzyme@MOF/MNP composites utilizing an aqueous-phase one-pot technique. Additional enzymes with substantial substrates of varying sizes may be immobilized with the help of this study. By adjusting the Ca/BDC concentration, the composites' size can be fine-tuned to meet the requirements of a wide range of applications, including improved reusability, greater mobility for delivering or contacting structured, acidic catalytic reaction requirements for optimized enzyme efficiency, and boosted aggregated composite stability [106].
Lin et al. prepared a magnetic hierarchically porous core-shell MOF that was successfully synthesized and used to immobilize amidase. The material comprises a core-shell structure, layered porosity, and high magnetic response. This amidase is immobilized on magnetic hierarchically porous MOF and demonstrates significant catalytic activity, improved thermal stability, and resistance to organic solvents. After 15 reuses, the immobilized enzyme restored \(98.2\,\%\) of its activity in (S)-4-fluorophenylglycine preparation. This work has inspired enzyme immobilization on hierarchically porous magnetic MOFs and may give a new way for synthesizing functional magnetic MOFs with hierarchical porosity [107].
A magnetic zeolite imidazolate framework-8 (iron oxide@ZIF-8@Trypsin) was synthesized by Zhong et al. using a one-pot synthesis method. Adjusting the pH of the reaction system caused ZIF-8 to grow around the citric acid, altered iron oxide, and immobilized trypsin. Iron oxide@ZIF-8@Trypsin was 2.6 times as active than free trypsin due to its high enzyme loading capacity and wide surface area. Thanks to its low magnetic reaction \((43\mathrm{\emu/g})\), it could be easily used and recycled. Potential for proteomic analysis is demonstrated using iron oxide@ZIF8@Trypsin as an immobilized enzyme micro-reactor (IMER) to efficiently digest proteins and complex human serum samples [108]. In recent studies, Li and colleagues used MOFs-based mimic enzymes that have been successfully manufactured using a straightforward and efficient process. In order to biomimetic the detection of glucose, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-88B(Fe)@MRGO was utilized [109].
Wang et al. used a-L-Rhamnosidase to destroy rhamnoside-containing biomass. Recombinant Rha mounted on magnetic MOFs hydrolyzed rutin. \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and PABA were covalently bonded to \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs covered in a polydopamine film, resulting in magnetic metal-organic frameworks. The enzyme was connected using EDC/NHS. Immobilized Rha@MOF had higher activity and lower Km than the free enzyme. 20 \(\mathrm{g/L}\) rutin converted to \(12.78\ \ \mathrm{g/L/h}\) isoquercitrin. Rha@MOF also showed increased reusability; the conversion rate was \(73.55\;\%\) after 30 cycles at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). These results indicated that magnetic MOF-immobilized enzymes could convert low-aqueous-solubility flavonoids [110]. Amari et al. used laccase for immobilized on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-101}\) (Cr). Despite being exposed to extreme temperatures and small pH, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) @MIL-101(Cr) laccase retained its immobilization ability and regenerated a significant amount of lost activity. Due to the high stability of the immobilized systems, laccase stored for 28 days retained \(88\;\%\) of its initial activity. At \(85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), residual activity was \(49\;\%\). Immobilized laccase biodegraded RB and AR dyes in water [111]. Wan et al. utilized in situ immobilization of a-glucosidase was achieved by assembling \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\). Immobilized a-glucosidase was more pH and temperature stable and reusable; after ten cycles, \(74\,\%\) of the overall enzyme activity was still present. The IC50 for acarbose was \(0.57\,\mathrm{M}\), whereas the Michaelis-Menten constant for the immobilized enzyme was \(0.47\ \mathrm{mM}\). Out of 14 TCMs, Rhei Radix et Rhizoma passed the immobilized enzyme screening. Three of Rhei Radix et Rhizoma's 10 constituents, gallic acid, \((+)\)-catechin, and epicatechin, were found to inhibit a-glucosidase at significantly higher concentrations [112].
## 6. Applications of MOFs-immobilized biocatalysts
## 6.1. Bio-decolorization of synthetic dyes
Synthetic dyes have gained extensive utilization in various industries. During the dyeing process, about \(10{-}15\ \ \%\) of dyes are approximated to discharge in the wastewater effluents, resulting in serious health and environmental problems due to their extensive structural diversity, recalcitrancy, and hazardous effects [113]. The presence of phenolic rings and various functional groups, such as NO, \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), OH, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), COOH, \(\mathrm{NR}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{NHR}\), in the structure of the dye are responsible for imparting toxicity [114]. Hence, the resultant dye-loaded wastewater effluents could be highly toxic, mutagenic, or carcinogenic to human life and aquatic entities [115]. In addition, extensive amounts of dyes can intensify water coloration, obstructing the penetration of sunlight at the water's surface, thus interfering with photosynthetic system and deteriorating the ecosystem. In this context, enzyme-based approaches have emerged as a prodigious solution to catalyze the biodegradation and biotransformation of various organic pollutants and textile dyes. Despite eco-friendliness and efficiency, free enzyme-based processes have been handicapped in industrial settings because of high cost, inadequate stability, and challenging recovery and reprocessing. Recent studies have demonstrated the applicability of magnetic MOFs-incorporated enzymes for removing of dyes, drugs, and other organic contaminants from water and wastewater [116,117]. Therefore, integrating MOFs with MNPs offers negligible diffusion restriction, thermal and chemical stabilization, large surface area, tunable porosity, and magnetic separation. [111] immobilized laccase onto amino-functionalized magnetic MOF \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\) to demonstrate its application for wastewater treatment. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\) biocatalyst manifested high stability to acidic pH and elevated temperatures with a substantial activity recovery. It efficiently catalyzed the degradation of Reactive Black 5 and Alizarin Red S dyes in water. Under the optimal reaction conditions, the sorption and biodegradation mechanism of magnetic nanobiocatalytic system mediated the complete elimination of Alizarin Red S and over \(80\;\;\%\) of Reactive Black 5. Magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) nanocomposites developed a facile method for one-step purification and immobilization of recombinant B. amyloliquefaciens laccase (Fig. 3). The immobilized laccase preserved over \(45\,\%\) of its original activity after incubating for \(6\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), whereas the free enzyme lost its activity under the same treatment conditions. It revealed excellent biocatalytic performance for dye decolorization, completely decolorizing indigo carmine dye in \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) at \(40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for five repeated cycles [118].
## 6.2. Biodegradation of phenolic compounds
Phenolic compounds are widely used as pesticides in various industries, including textile, plastics, and leather [119]. Nevertheless, the phenolic residues from these industries have been recognized as a threat to the environmental matrices and organisms [98]. They are included in the list of high-priority pollutants by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA), necessitating that the phenol contents in drinking water should be less than \(1~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}\) [120]. Therefore, searching for promising methods to remove such highly toxic, low-concentration, and recalcitrant pollutants is highly important from an environmental perspective. The commonly used chemical oxidation, physical adsorption, and microbiological-based treatment approaches are laborious and may generate secondary pollution when applied to remove phenolic contaminants [121]. In this juncture, the enzymatic approach has stepped up as a valuable bio-tool for removing phenolic pollutants, with potential advantages of environmentally friendly operational conditions, non-toxicity of by-products, and outstanding degradation efficacy [122]. [123], for the first time, encapsulated laccase enzyme into a MOF material, zeolitic metal azolate framework-7 (MAF-7), using a simple and mild synthetic method to develop catalytically active and affording
magnetically responsive MAF-7/Lac nanobio-composites (Fig. 4). After that, MNPs were introduced into the Lac@MOM to prepare Lac/ MNP@MOM nanobiocatalytic system. MAF-7, with potent pH buffering
capacity and better hydrophilicity, showed excellent compatibility with laccase, preserving the enzyme activity following immobilization process. Lac/MNP@MOM demonstrated high activity at extreme pH,
elevated temperature, organic solvents, and simplified recovery. It catalyzed \(97\ \%\) biodegradation of 2,4-dichlorophenol within \(24~\mathrm{h}\) and retained over \(80~\%\) of its activity after 9 successive operational cycles, offering the prospects in degrading active fungicide residues in leather manufacturing units.
Functional groups can act as a bridge between MOFs and biomacromolecules. For example, Xie and coworkers fabricated boronic acid-coated \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{PVP}/\mathrm{PEI}@\mathrm{MOF}(\mathrm{B})\) with high sensitivity and selectivity for glycopeptide enrichment [124]. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-COOH@MIL-101 were fabricated for selective enrichment of protein biomarkers from bacterial cell lysates [125]. Therefore, the integration of functional moieties to MOF structures can also be utilized for enzyme and biomolecule immobilization. For the first time, the amino group was coated onto functionalized magnetic MOF to synthesize \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-NH2@MIL-101(Cr) which was then used for laccase immobilization by covalent binding or adsorption and applied to catalyze the removal of 2,4-dichlorophenol (Fig. 5). Results revealed that adsorption of the enzyme onto \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-NH2@MIL-101(Cr) resulted in rapid elimination of 2,4-dichlorophenol in the first hour, eventually reaching \(87\ \%\) . After the reaction completion, the immobilized derivative was readily separated from the complex mixture by applying a magnet that indicates its considerable potential in wastewater remediation [89].
## 6.3. Removal of endocrine-disrupting compounds
Endocrine-disrupting compounds (EDCs) have currently received much apprehension from an environmental perspective because of their hostile impacts on humans and animals [126]. These heterologous compounds are known to have extensive types, including bisphenol A, phthalates, alkylphenols, estrogens, and polybrominated diphenyl ethers. Among these EDCs, alkylphenol polyethoxylated (APEO), including mainly nonylphenol polyethoxylated (NPEO) and octylphenol polycarboxylate (OPEO), have gained supreme attention and widely detected EDCs in the aquatic systems. Due to their extensive utilization in leather, rubber, paper, and textile industries as imperative components of detergents, emulsifiers, wetting agents, and dispersants, these are discharged into rivers, lakes, soil, and coastal waters, creating severe environmental contamination. It can be intensified by the food chain, leading to a number of human health-related disorders, such as hormonal imbalance, reproductive system, metabolic, immunological, and neurological disorders [127,128]. Commonly used physicochemical
methods for APEO treatment necessitate high energy consumption and induce secondary pollution. Integrating with other wastewater techniques, bioremediation is regarded as a suitable alternative with potential applicability. Recent studies have demonstrated the aptitude of Trametes versicolor and its ligninolytic enzyme consortium to catalyze the degradation of APEO representatives [129]. Wang et al. [130] prepared an amino-functionalized magnetic core-shell structure MOF, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\)-NH2@MIL-100 (Fe), by a convenient strategy and applied it to immobilize Trametes versicolor laccase via adsorption and covalent bonding (Fig. 6). The magnetically immobilized biocatalyst showed elevated activity recovery and augmented stability retaining over \(70\ \%\) of its primary activity after one month of storage. It catalyzed the complete removal of environmental pollutant OPEO and over \(98\ \%\) NPEO within \(200\ \mathrm{min}\) . After four consecutive cycles, it still achieved \(46.17\ \%\) and \(63.42\ \%\) elimination efficiency for NPEO and OPEO, respectively, indicating its usefulness in wastewater remediation. In a previous study, polyurethane foam-incorporated Trametes versicolor reduced the NPEO concentration over 18 days in an acidic nutrient solution [129]. After 90 days, \(71.3\,\%\) and \(96.3\,\%\) of NPEO were removed Gymnopus luxurians and Hypholoma fasciculare fungal strains, respectively, and the degradation was attributed to the secretion of MnP and laccase [131]. In the presence of excess metal mixtures, Hung et al. [132] tested the applicability of a purified recombinant enzyme for OPEO removal. After \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) of treatment, \(40\;\%\) and \(90\;\%\) of OP9EO and OP15EO were recorded to be eliminated, respectively, however, the process was challenging to implement. Therefore, the development of immobilized enzymes opens new avenues for removing organic pollutants in wastewater treatment.
## 6.4. Detection and degradation of herbicides
shown that frequent exposure to even trace levels of AOPP herbicide residues might induce DNA impairment in non-target organisms [138] and lung and liver injuries in humans and rats, respectively [139,140]. In addition, the herbicide residues were also found to be harmful to rotation crops, causing food safety risks [141]. Thus, developing a monitoring platform with excellent specificity, sensitivity, and reliability is required to rapidly determine herbicide residues in the biological and environmental samples.
The currently used herbicide detection techniques highly depend on the reliability and sensitivity of sophisticated analytical instrumentations, such as UV–vis absorption, GC–MS, and HPLC–MS [142,143]. Nevertheless, these methods are costly, time-laborious, and complicated regarding sample preparation and skilled operators. Enzyme-assisted electrochemical biosensing systems have loomed as prodigious approaches to meet the increasing demand for low-cost, rapid, consistent, and one-site detection of herbicide residues in samples [144]. Among four different types of biomolecules (DNA, aptamer, antigens or antibodies, and enzyme)-based biosensors, enzymatic biosensors are of particular interest in detecting pesticide residues given their exquisite selectivity and high activity under ambient environmental conditions. [145] introduced an easy one-pot method to construct an mZIF-8-based magnetic multi-enzymatic system concurrently encapsulating the alcohol oxidase (AOx) and AOPP herbicide hydrolase QpeH in aqueous solution for the detection of AOPP herbicides (Fig. 7). In this system, a sequential completion of a three-step cascade reaction in a single MNP QpeH/AOx@mZIF-8 realized swift recognition of a very low concentration of AOPP herbicides via the naked eye, circumventing the diffusional restrictions, intermediates decomposition, and the tiresome washing procedures. The newly constructed QpeH/AOx@mZIF-8 magnetic composite initially hydrolyzed
herbicides by means of embedded QpeH into corresponding acids and alcohol and then catalyzed the oxidation of latter by AOx producing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Using \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), the \(o\)-phenylenediamine (OPD) was immediately oxidized by the peroxidase-like catalytic activity of mZIF-8 to produce 2,3-diaminophenazine (an orange-colored product). QpeH/AOx@mZIF8-based biosensor presented the utmost sensitivity toward quizalofop-P-ethyl (Qpe) with a detection limit of \(8.2~\upmu\mathrm{M}\). The practicality of this biosensor was substantiated by quantitative analysis of Qpe residues in field soils and industrial wastewater. Easy separation, durable storage stability (50 days), and repeated usability (10 cycles) render QpeH/ AOx@mZIF-8 as a promising candidate for efficient and affordable sensing of herbicides in real environmental and biological samples.
## 6.5. Biodiesel production
Currently, worldwide biodiesel is mainly achieved via alkaline-assisted methanolysis [146]. Nevertheless, the oil feedstock used in this process must be refined to eliminate free fatty acids that significantly increase the processing cost and result in the loss of some fractions of feedstock [146,147]. The lipase-mediated biocatalytic process has garnered much attention for biodiesel production due to a number of advantageous features, such as minimal energy necessities, working under mild conditions, easier product retrieval, and utilization of unrefined oil [148]. Though lipases from various origins can catalyze the transesterification of triglycerides, fungal and bacterial-derived lipases, mainly from Thermomyces lanuginosus and Candida antarctica are widely applied to produce biodiesel. For the cost-effective deployment of enzymes for biodiesel preparation, lipases should be used in insolubilized form for efficient reusability and easy retention. Poor operational steadiness elevated mass transfer restraint, and by-product glycerol adsorption tendency onto the supporting matrix are the major problems in industrializing enzymatic systems [148]. These challenges can be addressed by suitable support materials with favorable pore sizes and surface features [20,149]. Given a larger surface area, porous support carriers promote superior loading, well-protection, and preservation of the enzymes [20]. Shomal et al. [150] executed lipase immobilization on the surface of three different MOFs (different pore sizes, structures, surface areas, and chemical properties), including ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and HKUST-1 and then employed them for biodiesel production. Adsorption data revealed that lipase was physically adsorbed on the surface of ZIF-8, and ZIF-67, whereas it was chemically attached to HKUST-1. The as-fabricated MOFs-incorporated biocatalyst was utilized to synthesize biodiesel in a co-solvent system. ZIF-67 and ZIF-80 presented better biocatalytic performance, resulting in \(90\%\) and \(88\%\) of conversions, whereas HKUST-1-immobilized enzyme demonstrated excellent
recycling efficiency because of potent chemical adoption.
