## 6.6. Green biosynthesis of sweeteners
6.6. Green biosynthesis of sweetenersHesperitin dihydrochalcone glucoside (HMGDC) is a non-nutritive and low-caloric sweetening agent with a sweetness level of 300–500 times that of sucrose. HMGDC is generally synthesized chemically or through enzymatic hydrolysis of hesperidin dihydrochalcone (HSDDC) into HMG following hydrogenation under alkaline conditions to acquire the target product HMGDC. However, the enzymatic hydrolysis efficacy is influenced by the poor water solubility of HSD. In addition, the resultant product necessitates additional separation and purification that results in low yield [153]. Many HSD hydrolytic by-products, including glucose, rutin, hesperidin, rhamnose, and HMG, interfere with the separation of the target product and also generate a massive amount of industrial effluents. Thus, it is of paramount concern to
develop a green and efficient method for the economical biosynthesis of HMGDC to ensure its large-scale production. Wang et al. [153] explored a green method for synthesizing HMGDC by immobilizing \(\upalpha\) -L-mannosidase on magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\) (Cr) NPs in the presence of MIL101 (Cr) as a precursor. The applicability of an immobilized enzymatic platform to synthesize valuable HMGDC sweetener was substantiated using HSDDC as a substrate (Fig. 9). In contrast to free enzyme, the nanobiocatalytic system showed high temperature tolerance, excellent stability, and recycling efficiency. After treatment for 4 days at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), it retained \(24.6\,\%\) of enzyme activity, and capable of converting
93.1\% of HSDDC, indicating promising prospect in the biocatalytic preparation of high value sweeteners.
## 6.7. Screening of ligands and inhibitors from natural herbs
Herbal medicines are a prolific source of biologically active molecules [154]. With the advancements in detection and separation technologies, significant interest has been diverted to isolating potent enzyme inhibitors from natural herbs. However, it is highly expensive and time-consuming to screen valuable constituents from plants and evaluate their potential biological activities. Different techniques, including ultrafiltration, ligand fishing, bio-affinity chromatography, and cellular membrane affinity chromatography, are proposed to explore a time-efficient and more convenient strategy [155]. Ligand fishing is considered an advantageous approach amid these methods owing to its high efficacy for coupling target proteins on solid supports and tolerance to fluctuating environmental conditions [156,157]. In recent years, various kinds of support materials, such as halloysite nanotubes, silica nanospheres, gold NPs, and MNPs, have been attempted for screening possible ligands from herbal extracts [158–161]. Nonetheless, the low protein loading and deactivation of immobilized enzymes is the major hindrance of ligand fishing, which are used to explore novel drug candidates from herbs. Chen et al. [162] designed MOF UiO-66-NH\(_2\) for immobilizing the porcine pancreatic lipase (PPL) through precipitation-crosslinking (Fig. 10). The resultant novel bio-platform PPL@MOF composite was used to screen lipase inhibitors for the enrichment and improving the ligand fishing techniques. Results revealed the harvesting of 12 lipase ligands from Prunella vulgaris L., whereas 12 compounds were identified by HPLCQ-TOF-MS/MS. The potential inhibiting activity of obtained ligands was further established by verifying the activity bioassay and molecular docking. The projected PPL@MOF based strategy was found efficient concerning facile preparation, high protein loading, and superior enzymatic activity. Therefore, this method offers an interesting platform for effective discovery of
numerous bioactive compounds from various natural herbal plants.
A rapid and simple screening strategy was proposed by integrating capillary electrophoresis analysis with immobilized \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase based enzymatic assay. To immobilize \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase, magnetic core-shell MOF nanocomposite (Fe\(_3\)O\(_4\)\@CS@ZIF-8) was constructed by a step-by-step assembly following in situ encapsulation of \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase in the crystal framework of ZIF-8 [112]. After fabricating highly dispersive Fe\(_3\)O\(_4\) magnetic microspheres by a modified solvothermal method, chitosan was anchored on the Fe\(_3\)O\(_4\) surface yielding Fe\(_3\)O\(_4\)\@CS core-shell architecture by electrostatic interactions between MNPs and chitosan using sodium tripolyphosphate as a crosslinking agent. The adsorption of zinc ions on the chitosan shell surface via coordinative interactions with 2-methyl imidazolate (organic ligand) results in forming of a porous ZIF-8 shield shell (Fig. 11). Immobilization caused a notable enhancement in pH and temperature tolerance ability of \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase and greatly enhanced its reusability with retention of over 70\% of original activity after ten repetitive runs. It was employed for screening of inhibitors from fourteen traditional Chinese medicines, where Rhei Radix et Rhizoma showed a compelling inhibition potential on \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase. These findings agree with the earlier reports, in which aloe-emodin, emodin, rhein, physcion, and chrysophanol from Rhei radix et Rhizoma have been identified as \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase inhibitors [163,164]. Moreover, Cyperi Rhizoma, Puerariae Lobatae Radix, and Notoginseng Radix et Rhizoma extract also displayed good inhibition activities. Earlier reports have substantiated the \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase inhibitory activity of scirpusin A and B from Cyperi Rhizoma, isoflavonoids from Puerariae Lobatae Radix, and panaxnotoginseng saponins from Notoginseng Radix et Rhizoma [165–167]. Amid the ten commercial constituents available in Rhei Radix et Rhizoma, (+)-catechin gallic acid and epicatechin presented the most potent inhibiting effect on the activity of \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase. The molecular docking studies further verified the modes and binding sites of these compounds with \(\upalpha\)-glucosidase. The results indicate the practicality of the Fe\(_3\)O\(_4\)\@CS@ZIF-8-based screening platform for the quick discovery of enzyme inhibitors from
complex TCMs extracts [112]. Fig. 11 and Table 1 enlist recent examples of enzyme-immobilized magnetic–MOFs for various biotechnological applications (Fig. 12).
## 7. Conclusive remarks and future prospects
Given robust thermal and chemical stability, MOFs are considered a prodigious support matrix to maintain enzyme stabilization against harsh and unfavourable environmental conditions. The development of various enzyme-linked MOF nanobio-hybrids represents a fascinating nanomaterial with unique functional attributes for a range of biotechnological applications. Explicitly, the amalgamation of MOFs with magnetic materials has gained incredible interest because it offers the integrated properties of porosity and magnetism to constitute new materials with substantial surface area and easier magnetic-assisted retrieval from the complex mixture. In addition, the response to external magnetic stimuli can offer "on-demand" applications in various sectors like controlled release drug delivery, sensing, catalysis, environmental remediation, and selective extraction and sequestration. Depending on the requisite application (i.e., delivery, sequestration, or catalysis), the constituents of magnetic framework composites can be engineered along with meticulous tuning of magnetic properties of the NPs following well established protocols.
In spite of plenty of advantageous features of magnetic-MOFs,
In spite of plenty of advantageous features of magnetic-MOFs, extensive efforts are still required to realize the real-time applications of magnetic MOFs-immobilized enzyme-based systems in biocatalysis. There is a lack of sufficient literature on designing and fabrication of nanostructured magnetic framework composites. Particularly, the controlled growth of MOF crystals on MNPs continues to be a challenge. Therefore, it is important to gain a comprehensive insight into the growth mechanism of MOF while taking into account the magnetic and functional attributes of various MNPs in biocatalysis. Many of the mesoporous MOFs are relatively unstable under varying pH, solvents, and water conditions for an extended period, which impedes their practical utility as support matrices for enzyme immobilization under real-time conditions. Thus, improving the structural steadiness of MOF is indispensable to comprehensively exploit this material for the development of industrially valuable enzyme-coupled nanobio-composite for biocatalytic applications. Post-synthetic modifications can be another applicable strategy to expand the application prospects of magnetic MOFs in various fields. Concerted efforts from multidisciplinary fields might unveil new opportunities in rationally designing magnetic MOFs-based hybrid nanomaterials with multifunctional attributes as a fascinating paradigm for enzyme and biomolecule immobilization. These new magnetic MOF-immobilized enzyme-based nano-biocatalytic systems can be exploited for potential applications in the field of biocatalysis and biotechnology.
